When `OrderService` creates `StripeGateway` internally with `new`, the two classes are permanently married. Every time the payment layer changes, the service that shouldn't care about it breaks.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    // Permanently coupled to Stripe — hardcoded, unhideable
    private final StripeGateway gateway = new StripeGateway();

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        gateway.charge(order.getTotal());
    }
}

// Problem 1: Unit testing — StripeGateway hits the real network.
//            No seam to inject a mock. You test Stripe, not OrderService.
// Problem 2: Swap to PayPal — must modify OrderService source code.
// Problem 3: Staging env — no way to inject SandboxGateway without code change.
// Problem 4: StripeGateway changes its constructor — OrderService compile error.
```

All four problems share the same root: `OrderService` decided what it needed instead of declaring what it needs.

Replacing `new StripeGateway()` with a constructor parameter flips control. `OrderService` now declares what it needs (`PaymentGateway` — a contract), and Spring provides the matching implementation.

```java
public interface PaymentGateway {
    void charge(double amount);
}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway; // depends on the contract, not Stripe

    // Spring finds a PaymentGateway bean and passes it here
    public OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        gateway.charge(order.getTotal());
    }
}

// Unit test — no Spring context, no network:
var service = new OrderService(amount -> System.out.println("mock: " + amount));

// Production: @Component StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway — Spring wires it.
// Staging:    @Profile("staging") @Component SandboxGateway — Spring picks the right one.
```

`OrderService` now depends on a contract. Swap providers, write tests, configure environments — the service class never changes. That's loose coupling in practice.

The Spring team recommends constructor injection for all mandatory dependencies, and the reasoning comes down to three concrete differences that show up in real code.

**Explicit contract** — the constructor signature tells you exactly what this class needs to function. A class with six field-injected `@Autowired` dependencies looks deceptively clean from the outside. Try to test it without Spring and you discover the hidden dependencies one NPE at a time. Constructor injection makes them visible from the first line.

**Immutability** — constructor-injected fields can be `final`. Once set, they never change. Field injection makes `final` impossible: Spring writes into the field after construction using `Field.setAccessible(true)`, and the JVM's memory model doesn't guarantee visibility for reflectively-written `final` fields. Immutable beans eliminate a class of race conditions in the shared singleton instances that power every Spring application.

**Testability without the container** — a constructor-injected class can be tested with `new OrderService(mockGateway)` in a plain JUnit test. No `@SpringBootTest`, no context startup overhead, no `@MockBean` setup. Field injection requires either `ReflectionTestUtils.setField()` or a full Spring context — both slower and more fragile.

Since Spring 4.3, if a class has exactly **one constructor**, `@Autowired` is not required. Spring discovers and uses it automatically. Combined with Lombok's `@RequiredArgsConstructor` — which generates a constructor for every `private final` field — constructor injection in modern Spring requires zero boilerplate.

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor  // generates constructor for all final fields
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    private final InventoryClient inventory;
    // No @Autowired — Spring 4.3+ uses the single constructor automatically
}
```

The most common injection **anti-pattern** is constructor over-injection — when a class takes more than 5-7 constructor parameters, it signals a **Single Responsibility Principle violation** rather than an injection style problem.

Refactoring over-injected constructors involves identifying cohesive groups of dependencies and extracting them into focused services. An `OrderService` with eight dependencies almost certainly handles too many concerns — billing, inventory, notifications, and auditing all crammed into one class.

```java
// BEFORE — 8 dependencies indicate SRP violation
public OrderService(OrderRepo repo, PaymentService pay,
    InventoryService inv, NotificationService notify,
    AuditService audit, PricingService price,
    TaxService tax, ShippingService ship) { ... }

// AFTER — extracted focused collaborators
public OrderService(OrderRepo repo,
    OrderFulfillment fulfillment,  // wraps inventory + shipping
    OrderBilling billing,          // wraps payment + pricing + tax
    OrderNotifier notifier) { ... }
```

The refactored version has four parameters, each representing a cohesive responsibility. The original eight are still injected — they just live in the right classes now.

**Circular dependency detection** differs by injection style. Constructor injection fails fast at startup with `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`, which is a feature — it forces you to fix the design. Field and setter injection hide circular dependencies at startup and may cause subtle runtime bugs when beans are partially initialized.

When you encounter a circular dependency, the fix is usually to **extract the shared concern** into a separate bean, use **domain events** to decouple the interaction, or apply the interface segregation principle so each bean depends on a narrow interface rather than a concrete class. Spring's `@Lazy` on one constructor parameter can break the cycle as a temporary workaround, but it masks a design problem that should be addressed.

The typical pattern is to mark one implementation as **`@Primary`** — the default that most injection points receive — and use `@Qualifier` only at injection points that need a specific alternative. This keeps most of your code clean while giving you precise control where it matters.

Consider an application with two notification channels. The **`EmailNotifier`** handles most cases, but certain services need to send SMS alerts instead. The `@Primary` annotation on `EmailNotifier` means every plain `Notifier` injection point gets email by default, without any qualifier needed.

```java
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String message);
}

@Primary  // default — most injection points get this
@Component
public class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send email */ }
}

@Component("smsNotifier")
public class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String message) { /* send SMS */ }
}
```

With these definitions, any service that declares `Notifier notifier` in its constructor gets `EmailNotifier` automatically. Only services that explicitly need SMS use `@Qualifier`.

```java
@Service
public class AlertService {
    // Gets EmailNotifier — @Primary wins when no qualifier is specified
    public AlertService(Notifier notifier) { ... }
}

@Service
public class EmergencyService {
    // Overrides @Primary — explicitly requests the SMS implementation
    public EmergencyService(@Qualifier("smsNotifier") Notifier notifier) { ... }
}
```

The **`@Qualifier` value must match the bean name** — defaulting to the class name with a lowercase first letter, or a custom name in `@Component("name")`. A common mistake is using the original casing — `@Qualifier("SmsNotifier")` instead of `@Qualifier("smsNotifier")` — which produces a confusing `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` at startup.

Beyond `@Primary` and `@Qualifier`, Spring has one more fallback: **field or parameter name matching**. If you declare `private Notifier smsNotifier`, Spring will match a bean named `smsNotifier` even without `@Qualifier`. This works but creates fragile coupling between variable names and bean registration — rename the field and injection silently breaks.

For applications with many qualified beans, **custom qualifier annotations** are the scalable approach. Instead of scattering string-based `@Qualifier("stripe")` across the codebase — where a typo silently fails — you create a type-safe annotation.

```java
@Qualifier
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.TYPE})
public @interface Stripe {}

@Stripe @Component
public class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { ... }

@Service
public class CheckoutService {
    // Compile-time safety — typos are caught by the compiler
    public CheckoutService(@Stripe PaymentGateway gateway) { ... }
}
```

The custom annotation is compile-time safe — if you rename it, every usage fails to compile. String-based qualifiers only fail at runtime. This pattern is common in production codebases with multiple database connections, messaging providers, or external API clients.

The `${property.key}` syntax resolves against Spring's `Environment`, which aggregates properties from `application.properties`, environment variables, system properties, and command-line arguments. Spring walks these sources in priority order and returns the first match.

Resolution happens at **bean creation time**, not at method call time. The value is injected once and cached in the field. If the property changes in the file after startup, the bean still holds the old value — there's no live reload unless you use Spring Cloud Config's `@RefreshScope`.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentClient {
    @Value("${payment.api.url}")
    private String apiUrl;

    @Value("${payment.timeout:5000}")
    private int timeoutMs;

    @Value("${payment.retries:3}")
    private int maxRetries;
}
```

The colon acts as a separator between the key and the default value. Watch for a subtle trap: there's **no space after the colon**. Writing `${key: default}` injects `" default"` with a leading space, which causes silent bugs in URLs and numeric parsing. Always use `${key:default}` without spaces.

While `${}` performs simple property lookup, `#{}` evaluates a **Spring Expression Language** expression. SpEL can call methods, access system properties, reference other beans, and perform arithmetic — which makes it powerful but also harder to debug when something goes wrong.

```java
@Value("#{systemProperties['java.version']}")
private String javaVersion;

@Value("#{T(Runtime).getRuntime().availableProcessors()}")
private int cpuCores;

@Value("#{'${allowed.origins}'.split(',')}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;
```

The combined syntax in the third example — `#{'${key}'.split(',')}` — first resolves the property placeholder, then applies SpEL to the result. This is the standard pattern for converting a comma-separated property into a `List`. Note the nesting: `${}` is inside `#{}`, never the reverse.

SpEL in `@Value` is evaluated at startup. If the expression fails — a null property, a class not found, a method that doesn't exist — you get a `SpelEvaluationException` and the application won't start. Keep expressions simple. If you need complex logic, move it to a `@PostConstruct` method where you can handle errors gracefully and log meaningful diagnostics.

The moment you have three or more `@Value` fields sharing a prefix — `${mail.host}`, `${mail.port}`, `${mail.username}` — you should migrate to `@ConfigurationProperties`. The benefits compound as the configuration group grows.

`@ConfigurationProperties` binds an entire prefix to a POJO. Fields map to properties automatically. Nested objects, lists, and maps work without SpEL. Most importantly, you can add `@Validated` and use **JSR-303 annotations** — `@NotBlank`, `@Min(1)`, `@Max(65535)` — to validate at startup. A missing required property fails with a clear message listing exactly what's wrong, instead of a generic `IllegalArgumentException`.

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "mail")
@Validated
public class MailProperties {
    @NotBlank private String host;
    @Min(1) @Max(65535) private int port = 587;
    private Duration connectionTimeout = Duration.ofSeconds(30);
}
```

Spring parses `Duration` values like `20s`, `5m`, or `PT30S` automatically — no conversion code needed. Spring Boot's annotation processor generates `spring-configuration-metadata.json` from `@ConfigurationProperties` classes, giving IDEs auto-completion for your custom properties. Your team stops guessing property names — the IDE tells them. This alone justifies the migration for any property group used by more than one developer.

The real power of Spring's stereotype model is that you can **compose your own**. Any annotation that is itself annotated with `@Component` (or any of its specializations) becomes a stereotype that component-scan discovers.

This is useful for encoding architecture rules. Instead of relying on developers to remember 'use `@Service` for business logic and `@Repository` for data access,' you create domain-specific stereotypes that carry those rules.

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Service  // inherits @Component behavior
@Transactional  // every bean gets transaction management
public @interface UseCase {}

// Usage — clear intent, automatic transactions
@UseCase
public class PlaceOrderUseCase {
    // @Transactional is inherited from @UseCase
    public Order execute(OrderRequest request) { ... }
}
```

The `@UseCase` annotation is discovered by component-scan (inherits from `@Service` which inherits from `@Component`), and every class annotated with it automatically gets `@Transactional` behavior. This pattern is common in **hexagonal architecture** codebases where the port/adapter boundary is enforced through custom stereotypes rather than package naming conventions.

`@SpringBootApplication` is a composed annotation that includes `@ComponentScan` with no explicit `basePackages` — which means it defaults to **the package of the class it's on**. This is why the Spring Boot convention is to place your main class in the root package of your project.

```java
// com.example.app.Application — scans com.example.app and all sub-packages
package com.example.app;

@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

With this structure, classes in `com.example.app.service`, `com.example.app.controller`, and `com.example.app.repository` are all discovered automatically. But `com.example.shared.utils` is outside the boundary — it won't be scanned.

To extend the scan, you can add explicit base packages. But be precise — scanning too broadly (like `com.example`) pulls in third-party library classes that happen to be annotated with `@Component`, which causes unexpected bean conflicts and slower startup.

The most practical scope problem is injecting a **request-scoped bean into a singleton**. A singleton is created once at startup — long before any HTTP request exists. If it holds a direct reference to a request-scoped bean, that reference points to nothing (or to a stale instance from a previous request).

Spring solves this with **scoped proxies**. When you declare `@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)`, Spring injects a CGLIB proxy instead of the real bean. The proxy delegates every method call to the current request's actual instance, looked up from the `RequestAttributes` at call time.

```java
@Component
@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class RequestContext {
    private String correlationId;
    // each HTTP request gets its own instance
}

@Service  // singleton
public class AuditService {
    private final RequestContext ctx;  // proxy, not real bean
    public AuditService(RequestContext ctx) { this.ctx = ctx; }
    // ctx.getCorrelationId() delegates to the current request's instance
}
```

Without the `proxyMode`, Spring would try to create the `RequestContext` at startup — outside any request — and throw a `BeanCreationException` with the message 'no thread-bound request found.' This is one of the most common Spring scope errors and the scoped proxy is the standard fix.

Beyond the five built-in scopes, Spring supports **custom scopes** which are essential for tenant isolation in multi-tenant SaaS applications and batch processing contexts.

**Implementing a custom scope** requires registering a `Scope` implementation that manages object lifecycle within your custom boundary. The following demonstrates how scoping and thread-local storage interact in practice.

```java
public class TenantScope implements Scope {
    private final ThreadLocal<Map<String, Object>> scopedObjects =
        ThreadLocal.withInitial(HashMap::new);

    @Override
    public Object get(String name, ObjectFactory<?> factory) {
        Map<String, Object> scope = scopedObjects.get();
        return scope.computeIfAbsent(name, k -> factory.getObject());
    }

    @Override
    public Object remove(String name) {
        return scopedObjects.get().remove(name);
    }
    // Register: beanFactory.registerScope("tenant", new TenantScope());
}
```

**Scope proxy internals** deserve attention. When you inject a request-scoped bean into a singleton, Spring creates a CGLIB proxy that delegates to the current thread's active scope. This means every method call on the proxy involves a scope lookup — roughly 50 nanoseconds overhead per call. For hot paths, cache the result in a local variable rather than calling proxy methods repeatedly.

**Refresh scope** (from Spring Cloud) is a production pattern worth understanding. Beans annotated with `@RefreshScope` are destroyed and recreated when `/actuator/refresh` is called. This enables dynamic configuration updates without restart, but beware: all in-flight requests using the old bean instance continue with stale state until completion.

The broken version injects the prototype directly. The fixed version uses `ObjectProvider` to defer instantiation to the point of use.

**Broken — prototype injected once, shared forever:**
```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ReportContext context;  // Injected once at startup

    public ReportService(ReportContext context) {
        this.context = context;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        context.reset();  // Attempt to reuse — NOT thread-safe
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Calling `reset()` doesn't make this safe. If two threads call `generate()` concurrently, they call `reset()` and `loadData()` on the same `context` object, interleaving their data.

**Fixed — fresh prototype on every call:**
```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider;

    public ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider) {
        this.contextProvider = contextProvider;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        ReportContext context = contextProvider.getObject();  // New instance
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Each call to `getObject()` creates a fresh `ReportContext`. No sharing, no reset, no thread-safety concerns. The key insight is that `ObjectProvider` wraps the bean factory's prototype creation — it's functionally equivalent to `applicationContext.getBean(ReportContext.class)` but without coupling your code to the ApplicationContext API.

Remember that Spring does **not** manage prototype bean destruction. The prototype instance you get from `getObject()` is yours to manage. If it holds resources (connections, file handles), you must close them explicitly — `@PreDestroy` won't be called on prototype beans.

The first two phases happen in quick succession but are **distinct operations**. During **instantiation**, Spring calls the constructor — either the no-arg constructor or the `@Autowired` constructor. If constructor injection is used, dependencies are resolved from the context and passed as arguments, making them available from the first line of the constructor body.

The crucial difference is **field and setter injection**. These happen after the constructor returns, during the dependency injection phase. `AutowiredAnnotationBeanPostProcessor` uses reflection to scan the newly created object for `@Autowired` and `@Value` annotations, then injects the values. This is why reading an `@Autowired` field inside the constructor returns `null` — the field hasn't been populated yet.

```java
@Component
public class ReportService {
    @Autowired private DataSource dataSource;

    public ReportService() {
        // dataSource is NULL here — injection hasn't happened yet
        // Any code that uses dataSource will throw NullPointerException
    }

    @PostConstruct
    void init() {
        // dataSource is available here — safe to use
        dataSource.getConnection(); // works
    }
}
```

This ordering is the single most important lifecycle detail for everyday development. The fix is either to use constructor injection (dependencies are available immediately) or move initialization logic to `@PostConstruct`.

When the `ApplicationContext` shuts down — either through `context.close()` or the JVM shutdown hook registered by `SpringApplication` — Spring calls destruction callbacks on **singleton beans** in reverse creation order.

The callback sequence mirrors initialization: `@PreDestroy` first, then `DisposableBean.destroy()`, then `@Bean(destroyMethod = "cleanup")`. Use `@PreDestroy` for releasing resources: closing connection pools, flushing caches, deregistering JMX beans.

```java
@Component
public class ConnectionPool {
    private final HikariDataSource ds;

    @PreDestroy
    void shutdown() {
        ds.close();  // release all pooled connections
        log.info("Pool closed gracefully");
    }
}
```

The critical exception is **prototype-scoped beans**. Spring does not track prototype instances after creation — it has no reference to call `@PreDestroy` on. If a prototype bean acquires resources, the calling code must close them explicitly. This is the lifecycle gap that catches developers who switch a singleton to prototype scope without adding manual cleanup.

When a bean uses multiple initialization mechanisms, Spring fires them in a **specific order**. Understanding this order matters because it determines which callback sees which state.

**1. `@PostConstruct`** fires first. This is a JSR-250 standard annotation — it works in any Jakarta EE container, not just Spring. Dependencies are fully injected when it runs.

**2. `InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet()`** fires second. This is Spring's older interface-based approach. It couples your code to the Spring API, so it's not recommended for new classes.

**3. `@Bean(initMethod = "setup")`** fires last. Specified as a string in the bean definition, it's called via reflection. This is the approach for third-party classes you can't annotate.

```java
@Component
public class CacheWarmer implements InitializingBean {
    @Autowired private ProductRepository repo;

    @PostConstruct
    void postConstruct() { log.info("1. @PostConstruct"); }

    @Override
    public void afterPropertiesSet() { log.info("2. afterPropertiesSet"); }

    // If registered via @Bean(initMethod="customInit")
    void customInit() { log.info("3. initMethod"); }
}
```

In practice, **use `@PostConstruct` for your own classes** and `initMethod` for third-party beans. There's rarely a reason to implement `InitializingBean` in new code.

When `ApplicationContext.close()` is called — either explicitly or through the JVM shutdown hook registered by `SpringApplication` — Spring iterates through all singleton beans in **reverse creation order** and calls their destruction callbacks.

The destruction sequence mirrors initialization: `@PreDestroy` first, then `DisposableBean.destroy()`, then `@Bean(destroyMethod = "cleanup")`. The reverse order ensures that beans are destroyed before the beans they depend on.

```java
@Component
public class MetricsReporter {
    private final ScheduledExecutorService scheduler;

    @PostConstruct
    void startReporting() {
        scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);
        scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::report, 0, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    @PreDestroy
    void stopReporting() {
        scheduler.shutdown();
        // Wait for in-flight reports to finish
        scheduler.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

The trap is **prototype beans**. Spring creates them on demand but never tracks them — `@PreDestroy` is never called. If you switch a singleton to prototype scope without adding manual cleanup, resources leak silently. Similarly, beans registered via `@Bean` have an implicit `destroyMethod` that Spring infers by looking for `close()` or `shutdown()` methods — set `@Bean(destroyMethod = "")` to disable this if the auto-detection causes problems.

The two methods in `BeanPostProcessor` run at different points in the bean lifecycle, and the distinction determines which features each hook can implement.

**`postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`** runs after dependency injection but before `@PostConstruct`. This is where Spring processes annotations that configure the bean — `@Autowired`, `@Value`, `@Resource`. The bean receives its dependencies in this phase.

**`postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`** runs after `@PostConstruct` and all initialization callbacks. This is where Spring creates **proxies**. The method can return a different object than the one it received — and that replacement becomes the bean that other components inject. `@Transactional`, `@Async`, `@Cacheable`, and AOP aspects all create proxies in this hook.

```java
@Component
public class TimingBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        // Runs before @PostConstruct
        return bean;  // Return the same bean, or a modified/replaced one
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        // Runs after @PostConstruct — can wrap in proxy
        if (bean.getClass().isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class)) {
            return createTimingProxy(bean);
        }
        return bean;
    }
}
```

Returning a different object from `postProcessAfterInitialization()` is how proxy-based features work. The original `OrderService` instance is created, initialized, then replaced by a proxy that adds transactional behavior around each method call. Every other bean that injects `OrderService` gets the proxy, not the original.

The most practical way to understand `BeanPostProcessor` is to build one. The following example creates a **custom `@Timed` annotation** that automatically logs how long each method takes — similar to what Micrometer or Spring AOP would do, but implemented from scratch to show the mechanism.

The processor scans every bean for methods annotated with `@Timed`. When it finds one, it wraps the **original bean in a CGLIB proxy** that intercepts method calls and measures execution time. This is the same pattern Spring uses internally for `@Transactional` and `@Cacheable`.

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface Timed {}

@Component
public class TimingPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String name) {
        boolean hasTimed = Arrays.stream(bean.getClass().getMethods())
            .anyMatch(m -> m.isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class));
        if (!hasTimed) return bean;

        // Return a proxy that wraps the original bean
        return Enhancer.create(bean.getClass(), (MethodInterceptor) (obj, method, args, proxy) -> {
            if (!method.isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class))
                return proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            long start = System.nanoTime();
            Object result = proxy.invokeSuper(obj, args);
            log.info("{}.{} took {}ms", name, method.getName(),
                (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000);
            return result;
        });
    }
}
```

The key insight is the **return value**. The `postProcessAfterInitialization` method returns either the original bean (no processing needed) or a new object (the proxy). Spring replaces the bean in the container with whatever this method returns. This is why `@Transactional` works — the bean registered in the container is a proxy, not the original class.

This pattern has a practical limitation worth noting. Because the proxy is created during `postProcessAfterInitialization`, it happens once at startup for singleton beans. The performance overhead of the proxy is negligible for most applications — CGLIB proxies add roughly 1-2 microseconds per method call. But if you're writing a post-processor that wraps thousands of beans, the startup time impact can be measurable. Profile before optimizing.

When you annotate a bean with `@Lazy`, Spring doesn't skip the bean definition — it still registers the bean in the container. What changes is the **instantiation timing**. Instead of creating the bean at startup, Spring injects a **proxy** that triggers real creation on the first method call.

```java
@Service
@Lazy
public class ReportEngine {
    public ReportEngine() {
        // This constructor runs on first use, not at startup
        loadHeavyResources();
    }
}

@Service
public class DashboardService {
    private final ReportEngine engine;

    // @Lazy on the parameter — creates a proxy for this injection point
    public DashboardService(@Lazy ReportEngine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;  // proxy, not real bean
    }

    public Report generate() {
        return engine.build();  // proxy triggers real creation here
    }
}
```

The `@Lazy` annotation can be placed on the bean class (affects all injection points) or on a specific injection point parameter (affects only that consumer). The parameter-level placement is more common because it gives fine-grained control — one service might need the bean eagerly while another can tolerate lazy initialization.

**Fix 1: Extract the shared concern.** If A needs B and B needs A, they likely share logic that belongs in a third class C. Both A and B depend on C instead of each other. This eliminates the cycle entirely and often improves cohesion.

**Fix 2: Replace one direction with events.** If A needs to notify B but B also needs to query A, replace the notification with a Spring event. A publishes, B listens. B can still depend on A for queries (one-directional), but A no longer depends on B.

```java
// Instead of: PaymentService → NotificationService → PaymentService
// Use events: PaymentService publishes, NotificationService listens
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;
    public void processPayment(Payment p) {
        // ... process ...
        events.publishEvent(new PaymentCompleted(p.getId()));
    }
}

@Component
public class NotificationListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onPayment(PaymentCompleted event) {
        // notification logic — no dependency on PaymentService
    }
}
```

**Fix 3: Interface segregation.** If B only needs one method from A, extract that method into an interface that A implements. B depends on the narrow interface, not on A directly. This often reveals that B didn't really need 'all of A' — it needed a specific capability.

**Why `@Lazy` is the wrong answer:** It masks the cycle by deferring creation. The code works, but the design problem remains. When someone removes the `@Lazy` annotation during refactoring (not knowing it was load-bearing), the `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException` returns.

When you call `this.internalMethod()` inside a Spring bean, `this` refers to the **original object**, not the proxy. The proxy sits between the caller and the bean — it only intercepts calls that arrive through the injected reference.

This is the most common source of 'why doesn't my `@Transactional` work?' bugs. A public method calls a private method annotated with `@Transactional` — the transaction never starts because the call never goes through the proxy.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validate(order);
        this.saveOrder(order);  // bypasses proxy — no transaction!
    }

    @Transactional
    public void saveOrder(Order order) {
        repository.save(order);
    }
}
```

The fix has three options, each with trade-offs.

**Option 1: restructure** — move `saveOrder()` to a separate bean so the call crosses the proxy boundary. This is the cleanest solution. 

**Option 2: self-injection** — inject the bean into itself (`@Autowired private OrderService self`) and call `self.saveOrder()`. This works because `self` is the proxy reference. **Option 3: AspectJ weaving** — switch from Spring AOP to compile-time AspectJ, which modifies the bytecode directly and doesn't rely on proxies. This is the nuclear option — use it only if you genuinely need self-invocation aspects.

The `execution()` designator matches based on method signatures. Its syntax looks complex but follows a fixed pattern:

`execution(modifiers? return-type declaring-type?.method-name(params) throws?)`

Each part accepts wildcards: `*` matches one segment, `..` matches zero or more. Let's break down the most common patterns.

```java
// All public methods in the service package
@Before("execution(public * com.example.service.*.*(..))")

// All methods returning void in any subpackage
@Before("execution(void com.example..*.*(..))")

// All methods named "find*" with any parameters
@Before("execution(* com.example..find*(..))")

// Methods taking exactly one String parameter
@Before("execution(* *..*(String))")

// Methods with no parameters
@Before("execution(* *..*())")
```

The `..` in `com.example..` matches any number of sub-packages. The `..` in `(..)` matches any number of parameters of any type. Without `..` for packages, only the exact package is matched — a common mistake when methods live in sub-packages.

A key performance note: avoid patterns that match too broadly. `execution(* *.*(..))` causes Spring to proxy **every bean** and intercept **every method call**. For a large application, this adds measurable latency to every operation. Scope your patterns to specific packages or use annotation-based pointcuts for precision.

The `@annotation()` designator is the safest approach for custom aspects because it's explicitly opt-in: only methods (or classes) carrying a specific annotation are matched.

```java
// Match any method annotated with @Timed
@Around("@annotation(com.example.annotation.Timed)")

// Match any method in a class annotated with @Audited
@Before("@within(com.example.annotation.Audited)")

// Bind the annotation value to an advice parameter
@Around("@annotation(timed)")
public Object time(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, Timed timed) {
    String metricName = timed.value();  // Access annotation attributes
}
```

When you use a **lowercase name** in the designator (`@annotation(timed)` instead of a fully-qualified class), Spring binds the annotation instance to a method parameter of that name. This gives you access to annotation attributes inside the advice — useful for configurable aspects where the annotation carries metadata like metric names or cache keys.

Combine pointcuts using `&&` (and), `||` (or), and `!` (not) for complex matching.

```java
@Pointcut("execution(* com.example.service..*.*(..))")
public void serviceLayer() {}

@Pointcut("@annotation(Transactional)")
public void transactional() {}

@Before("serviceLayer() && !transactional()")
public void logNonTransactional(JoinPoint jp) {
    // Fires on service methods that are NOT @Transactional
}
```

Extract reusable pointcuts as `@Pointcut`-annotated methods. This avoids duplicating the same expression across multiple advice methods and gives the pointcut a descriptive name. When the package structure changes, you update one `@Pointcut` method instead of hunting through every advice annotation.

`REQUIRES_NEW` and `NESTED` both provide rollback isolation from the outer transaction, but they work through fundamentally different mechanisms with different failure characteristics.

**`REQUIRES_NEW` suspends the outer transaction** entirely and opens a new database connection (or borrows from the pool). The inner transaction commits or rolls back on its own connection. If the inner transaction commits before the outer fails, the inner commit is **permanent** — the database cannot undo it when the outer transaction rolls back on a different connection. This is exactly what audit logging needs: the log entry persists even if the business operation fails.

**`NESTED` uses a savepoint** within the same physical connection. When the inner method begins, Spring (via JDBC) issues `SAVEPOINT inner_tx`. If the inner method throws, Spring rolls back to the savepoint — undoing only the inner work. The outer transaction can catch the exception and continue. But if the outer transaction fails, the rollback undoes everything back to the start — including work done within the nested transaction, since it's all on the same connection.

```java
// REQUIRES_NEW: audit log survives business transaction failure
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void writeAuditLog(AuditEntry entry) {
    auditRepo.save(entry);  // commits on its own connection
}

// NESTED: optional enrichment — failure doesn't kill the outer operation
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)
public void enrichWithExternalData(Order order) {
    // if this fails, caller catches the exception and continues without enrichment
    ExternalData data = externalApiClient.fetch(order.getId());
    order.setExternalMetadata(data);
}
```

**The resource cost of `REQUIRES_NEW`** matters in high-throughput systems. Each `REQUIRES_NEW` call borrows a connection from the pool for the duration of the inner transaction, while the outer transaction still holds its connection. For a service that processes 100 requests/sec with 5 `REQUIRES_NEW` audit calls each, you're using 6× the connections you'd expect. Connection pool exhaustion under load is the failure mode. `NESTED` avoids this: one connection, one transaction, multiple savepoints.

Spring's event system has three components. The **publisher** calls `applicationEventPublisher.publishEvent(event)`. The **`ApplicationEventMulticaster`** discovers all listeners registered for that event type. Each **listener** receives the event and processes it.

Since Spring 4.2, events can be any object — you don't need to extend `ApplicationEvent` anymore. And listeners are just methods annotated with `@EventListener`, making the wiring zero-boilerplate. The following example shows the complete event flow — a domain event POJO, a publishing service, and a consuming listener.

```java
// Event — plain POJO, no Spring dependency
public record OrderPlaced(String orderId, BigDecimal total) {}

// Publisher — injects Spring's event infrastructure
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher publisher;
    public OrderService(ApplicationEventPublisher publisher) {
        this.publisher = publisher;
    }
    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        repository.save(order);
        publisher.publishEvent(new OrderPlaced(order.getId(), order.getTotal()));
    }
}

// Listener — runs synchronously in the publisher's thread by default
@Component
public class InventoryListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlaced event) {
        inventoryService.reserve(event.orderId());
    }
}
```

The listener runs on the same thread and in the same transaction as `placeOrder()`. If `reserve()` throws an exception, the entire transaction rolls back — including the `repository.save()`. This is often not what developers intended.

The production pattern for events that should only fire after a successful commit is **`@TransactionalEventListener`**. It binds the listener to a specific transaction phase — most commonly `AFTER_COMMIT` — so the event processing only happens if the transaction succeeds.

This solves the email notification problem. You don't want to send an 'Order Confirmed' email if the order transaction later rolls back due to a payment failure. With `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`, the email only sends after the commit is final.

```java
@Component
public class NotificationListener {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void sendConfirmation(OrderPlaced event) {
        // Only runs if the transaction committed successfully
        emailService.send(event.orderId());
    }
}
```

There's a subtle trap: if you use `@TransactionalEventListener` and no transaction is active when the event is published, **the listener never fires** — silently. Spring doesn't throw an error; it just discards the event. Add `fallbackExecution = true` if you need the listener to fire even outside a transaction context.

For true **async event handling**, combine `@Async` with `@EventListener`. This runs the listener on a separate thread pool, fully decoupling execution. But `@Async` requires an `@EnableAsync` configuration and a properly sized thread pool — without it, tasks queue up on the default `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` which creates unbounded threads.

Writing a custom `Condition` is straightforward — implement the interface, check whatever you need from the `ConditionContext`, and return a boolean. The context gives you access to the **`Environment`** (properties), the **`BeanDefinitionRegistry`** (existing beans), the **`ClassLoader`** (classpath checks), and the **`ResourceLoader`** (file existence).

A practical use case is **feature flags**. Instead of cluttering your code with `if (featureEnabled)` checks, you conditionally register the entire bean graph for a feature based on a property. The condition evaluates at bean definition time — before any bean is instantiated — so disabled features have zero runtime cost.

```java
public class FeatureEnabledCondition implements Condition {
    @Override
    public boolean matches(ConditionContext context,
                           AnnotatedTypeMetadata metadata) {
        // Read the feature name from the annotation attributes
        Map<String, Object> attrs = metadata
            .getAnnotationAttributes(ConditionalOnFeature.class.getName());
        String feature = (String) attrs.get("value");
        return "true".equals(
            context.getEnvironment().getProperty("feature." + feature + ".enabled"));
    }
}

@Target({ElementType.TYPE, ElementType.METHOD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Conditional(FeatureEnabledCondition.class)
public @interface ConditionalOnFeature {
    String value();
}

// Usage — bean only exists when feature.dark-mode.enabled=true
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnFeature("dark-mode")
public class DarkModeConfig {
    @Bean public ThemeService themeService() { return new DarkThemeService(); }
}
```

When `feature.dark-mode.enabled` is absent or `false`, the entire `DarkModeConfig` class is skipped — no `ThemeService` bean exists in the context. This is cleaner than wrapping business logic in conditionals because the bean graph itself reflects the application's active features.

The `ConditionContext` is the key to writing powerful conditions. Beyond property checks, you can inspect the classpath with `context.getClassLoader()`, check existing bean definitions with `context.getRegistry()`, and read resource files with `context.getResourceLoader()`. Spring Boot's built-in conditions use all of these — `@ConditionalOnClass` checks the classloader, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` checks the registry.

