The `@Profile` annotation controls which beans exist in which environment. When placed on a `@Component`, `@Configuration`, or `@Bean` method, Spring only registers that bean if the named profile is active. This is how you swap implementations between environments **without changing code or rebuilding the artifact**.

The most common pattern is environment-specific data sources. In development you use an embedded H2 database with fast startup. In production you connect to PostgreSQL with connection pooling and specific timeout settings.

```java
@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {
    @Bean @Profile("dev")
    public DataSource devDataSource() {
        return new EmbeddedDatabaseBuilder()
            .setType(EmbeddedDatabaseType.H2)
            .addScript("schema.sql").build();
    }

    @Bean @Profile("prod")
    public DataSource prodDataSource() {
        HikariConfig config = new HikariConfig();
        config.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/app");
        config.setMaximumPoolSize(20);
        return new HikariDataSource(config);
    }
}
```

Both beans have the same type (`DataSource`), but only one will exist in the container based on the active profile. The `@Profile("!prod")` negation syntax is useful for registering beans in every profile except production — common for debug tooling and test data seeding.

