While SpEL supports a rich expression language, production codebases typically use only a few patterns. The three most common are **bean references**, **property transformations**, and **conditional defaults**.

```java
// 1. Reference another bean's property
@Value("#{configService.getMaxRetries()}")
private int maxRetries;

// 2. Transform a property — split CSV into a List
@Value("#{'${allowed.origins}'.split(',')}")
private List<String> allowedOrigins;

// 3. Conditional default — use environment-aware logic
@Value("#{systemProperties['os.name'].contains('Windows') ? 'C:/tmp' : '/tmp'}")
private String tempDir;
```

Notice the nesting in pattern 2: `${}` resolves the property first, then `#{}` applies SpEL to the result. The order matters — `${}` is always inside `#{}`, never the reverse. This combined syntax is the standard pattern for converting comma-separated properties into Java collections.

SpEL can also access **static methods and constants**: `#{T(java.lang.Math).PI}` returns 3.14159, and `#{T(java.util.UUID).randomUUID().toString()}` generates a UUID. The `T()` operator provides access to any class on the classpath.
